{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791600753,"nonce":"TEmmRP741VGa4KcdBcgZ0oKusNo=","title":"Reachability over deferred library shells walks the module once per layer of shells","message":"TieredRuntime's reachable_with_body_sources, run twice per compile, took reachable_function_ids and then looped: the direct callees of every shell reached so far became the roots of a fresh reachable_from_roots, whose result was merged in, until no new shell turned up. Each pass started from an empty set, so it walked again whatever the earlier passes had reached, and seeded the module's whole address-taken scan again on its first indirect call; the walks hashed with SipHash. In a Lua fib start-up profile (ZYLUA_PROFILE_STARTUP=2000, Apple M-series) the function held 283 of ~3,500 samples per iteration (8%).\n\nOne walk now takes a shell's callees from its source as it reaches the shell (dce::reachable_function_ids_with), and resolves the names a shell's body calls by from its ShellRefs as a body's are; the walks hash with the id hasher and hand back std sets.\n\nStart-up instructions against d986224d (run stopped once start-up is done, PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3): Lua 17 benchmarks 29.65G -\u003e 29.24G (-1.4%; small kernels -3 to -4.5%); Python go/nbody/richards/float/json/fft 28.74G -\u003e 28.44G (-1.0%).","files":null}]}